description:
Goal: implement a compatibility shim and concrete interface examples across TypeScript/Python/Go that let legacy sync clients incrementally consume streaming responses, invoke tools (function calls), and reliably produce typed JSON chunks (structured outputs) without breaking older SDK surface areas.

Background: We continue to expand the runtime streaming+tools surface. Many application teams need: (1) a minimal breaking-change path from sync->streaming; (2) a deterministic JSON emission mode for structured outputs that can be validated as partial bytes arrive; and (3) a single, well-documented compatibility shim for TypeScript, Python, and Go SDKs. This ticket is the engineering work to design, implement, and ship the shim + canonical examples and tests.

Non-goals: full SDK refactor, adding new public sync-only endpoints, or major performance micro-optimizations (those will be follow-ups).

Design summary:
- Provide an "IncrementalInvocation" shim that wraps a streaming invocation and exposes:
  * a sync-like iterator for older clients (pull-based),
  * an async streaming event emitter for new clients (push-based),
  * a typed JSON reassembly helper that tolerates partial tokens and emits schema-validated objects as they are complete.
- The shim will live under each SDK package as a small compatibility module (e.g., @redwood/sdk/incremental for TS, redwood.incremental for Python, redwood/incremental for Go).
- Backcompat policy: defaults to non-breaking behavior; new APIs opt-in via import path or config flag ("enableIncrementalShim: true").

API examples (canonical snippets to include in docs and tests):

TypeScript (consumer-facing example):
const invocation = client.incremental.invoke({
  model: 'rw-large-2',
  input: prompt,
  tools: [summarizeTool],
  structured_schema: mySchema,
});

// Pull-based (sync-style) consumption for legacy codepaths
for await (const chunk of invocation.iterateSyncLike()) {
  if (chunk.type === 'structured') {
    // "chunk.payload" is a fully-assembled JSON object validated against mySchema
    handleStructured(chunk.payload);
  } else if (chunk.type === 'delta') {
    appendDelta(chunk.text);
  } else if (chunk.type === 'tool_request') {
    // shim exposes a helper to call tool and return result into the stream
    const res = await invocation.callTool(chunk.toolName, chunk.args);
    invocation.provideToolResult(chunk.requestId, res);
  }
}

// Push-based (modern async) usage
invocation.on('event', (e) => {
  // e.type in ['delta','structured','tool_request','end','error']
});
invocation.start();

Python (consumer-facing example):
from redwood import Client
client = Client()
inv = client.incremental.invoke(model='rw-large-2', input=prompt, tools=[summarize_tool], structured_schema=my_schema)

# sync-compat iterator (returns dicts for structured output)
for item in inv.iter_sync_like():
    if item['type'] == 'structured':
        process_structured(item['payload'])
    elif item['type'] == 'tool_request':
        result = inv.call_tool(item['tool_name'], item['args'])
        inv.provide_tool_result(item['request_id'], result)

Go (consumer-facing example):
client := redwood.NewClient(ctx)
inv := client.Incremental().Invoke(ctx, redwood.InvokeParams{Model: "rw-large-2", Input: prompt, Tools: []redwood.Tool{summarizeTool}})
// channel-based consumption for Go idioms
for ev := range inv.Events() {
    switch ev.Type {
    case redwood.EventTypeStructured:
        var obj map[string]interface{}
        obj = ev.StructuredPayload
        handleStructured(obj)
    case redwood.EventTypeToolRequest:
        res := inv.CallTool(ctx, ev.ToolName, ev.Args)
        inv.ProvideToolResult(ev.RequestID, res)
    }
}

Implementation notes / trade-offs:
- Reassembly and schema validation will run on the client side to avoid coupling streaming framing to a single server-side schema validator. We'll provide a lightweight incremental JSON accumulator that tolerates token boundaries (handles partial escape sequences and nested objects).
- For JS/TS we will leverage a small WASM-backed JSON streaming validator for common schemas to keep CPU down on large streams (optional, enabled by structured_schema).
- For Python and Go, the initial implementation will be pure-Python / pure-Go incremental parsers with targeted optimizations later.
- Security: tool invocation carry a request ID and signature; the shim will validate the tool_request envelope and ensure the client cannot spoof tool results.

Acceptance criteria:
- Unit tests: >95% coverage in shim modules for each language.
- Integration tests: upstream runtime streaming harness exercising: tool_request lifecycle, partial structured-object reassembly, and sync-style iteration.
- Example apps: three minimal examples (TS, Py, Go) in the SDK repo under examples/incremental that compile and run in CI.
- Docs: canonical guide in docs/sdk/incremental.md with code snippets above and migration notes for existing customers.
- Backcompat safety: default behavior unchanged for existing clients unless they import the incremental module or set the opt-in flag.

Risks / Mitigations:
- Risk: partial-object parsing edge cases for deeply nested schemas -> Mitigation: add fuzz tests and a fallback reassembly mode that buffers until balanced braces appear for pathological cases.
- Risk: performance overhead on small messages -> Mitigation: add heuristics to bypass reassembly when structured_schema is absent.
- Risk: cross-language semantic drift -> Mitigation: cross-language conformance tests and example parity checks in CI.

Next steps / checklist:
- [x] Design doc (linked).
- [ ] TypeScript shim PR: open, draft (PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/7891).
- [ ] Python shim PR: in feature branch.
- [ ] Go shim PR: initial API done, waiting on tests.
- [ ] Examples + docs PR.

Notes: We should coordinate with Runtime team for stable streaming event envelope (ENG-50421) — there is a in-flight proposal to add explicit "structured_complete" markers; if that lands we can simplify reassembly.
